AI agents now write trading strategies and ask for capital. AlphaCourt is a checkpoint, or gateway, between the agent and the broker. Connected over the Model Context Protocol, it holds each request for capital and puts the strategy on trial: a prosecutor, a defence and a judge argue over the output of deterministic tests, and an \emph{exhibit rule}, enforced in code, strikes any finding that cites no damaging test.
On simulated cases with known truth, a court run by the small open model Qwen3 8B stopped {{e1.court.small.guilty_stopped}} flawed strategies and deployed {{e1.court.small.innocent_deployed}} honest ones.
Without the rule, the same model deployed {{e1.single.small.innocent_deployed}} honest strategies reviewing alone, and {{e1.single_exhibits.small.innocent_deployed}} when shown the test results. Evidence is not enough; the rule is what lets honest work through.
Applying fixed pass/fail thresholds to the same tests, with no model at all, does as well. The court adds a readable trial record, but its stated reasons are often wrong, and a court run by Gemma 3 12B stopped only {{e1.court.mid.guilty_stopped}} flawed strategies.
Everything is simulated and no real order is placed.
